from pathlib import Path
from runpy import run_path
g=run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('author-tools-generator.py')))
L=g['lesson']; C=g['course']
def B(*a): return L(*a,language='bash')

C('docker','Docker packages programs and their needed files into images. Containers run from those images. We will inspect small examples and practise only on a local learning machine.','Docker commands need a local Docker installation. Pulling images needs internet access and storage. These examples are for a disposable practice environment, not this website\'s runtime.','https://docs.docker.com/get-started/',[
B('Images and containers','An image is a packaged starting point. A container is a running or stopped instance created from an image.','docker version','The command reports Docker client and engine versions when the engine is available. It does not run a container.','Explain an image as a recipe and a container as one use of that recipe.','Installing a command-line client alone does not mean the Docker engine is running.','Which one is an instance?','A container','An image recipe'),
B('Run a small example','docker run creates and starts a container. --rm asks Docker to remove that container after it exits.','docker run --rm hello-world','Docker may download the image, then its small program prints a greeting and exits. The downloaded image can remain locally.','Identify which option removes the exited container.','--rm removes the container after exit; it does not delete the image.','Does --rm remove the downloaded image?','No','Yes'),
B('See local images','An image list shows packages stored on your machine. A tag is a readable label for an image version.','docker image ls','The table shows local image names, tags and sizes. The list depends on what has been downloaded or built.','Find the hello-world image after the previous lesson.','A mutable tag can later point to another image; it is not a permanent content identity.','What is a tag?','A readable image label','A running process'),
B('List containers','docker ps lists running containers. Adding -a also shows stopped containers that were not automatically removed.','docker ps\ndocker ps -a','The second list can include stopped containers. Containers run with --rm may no longer appear after exit.','Compare the two lists without changing any containers.','A stopped container and a deleted container are different states.','Which command includes stopped containers?','docker ps -a','docker image ls'),
B('Name a practice container','A container name gives you a clear target for later inspection. The -d option runs it in the background.','docker run -d --name club-web -p 127.0.0.1:8080:80 nginx:stable','The container starts a local web server. Port 8080 on the local machine forwards to port 80 in the container.','Open localhost:8080 on the same machine if you chose to run the example.','Use a fresh name and bind practice ports to 127.0.0.1 so they are not exposed on every network interface.','Which host port is used?','8080','80'),
B('Read container logs','Logs record messages written by a container\'s program. They help explain what it is doing or why it stopped.','docker logs club-web','The command prints available logs for the named practice container. Reading logs does not restart it.','Visit the practice page and look for a new request entry.','Do not put secrets into logs; logs are often copied elsewhere.','What does docker logs show?','Program messages','The contents of every host file'),
B('Inspect container details','inspect returns detailed configuration and state. A format expression can select a small part of the result.','docker inspect --format="{{.State.Status}}" club-web','The result describes the container state, such as running. It depends on the current container.','Compare the full inspect output with the selected field.','Inspect only containers you own or have permission to examine.','What field is selected here?','State.Status','Every environment value'),
L('Read a Dockerfile','A Dockerfile describes how to build an image. FROM chooses a base; COPY adds a file from the build folder.','FROM nginx:stable\nCOPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html','The planned image starts with Nginx and adds your static index.html. Put the Dockerfile beside that file.','Write a tiny index.html that says Reading club.','Do not COPY secret files into an image; image layers can retain them.','What does COPY add?','A file from the build context','A login to Docker Hub',language='dockerfile'),
B('Build a local image','docker build reads a Dockerfile and its build context. -t gives the resulting image a tag.','docker build -t club-page:learning .','The dot means the current directory is the build context. Build this only inside the intended practice folder.','Identify where the Dockerfile and index.html must be.','A large or secret-filled build context can include files you did not intend to send to the builder.','What does the final dot mean?','Current directory as build context','Delete the current directory'),
L('Keep unwanted files out','A .dockerignore file excludes matching files from the build context. This keeps temporary or private files out of a build.','.git\nnode_modules\n.env\n*.log','These patterns exclude Git history, installed packages, a common environment file and log files.','Add a pattern for a local scratch directory.','Ignoring a file in Git and ignoring it in a Docker build are separate settings.','Which file controls build-context exclusions?','.dockerignore','.gitconfig'),
L('Volumes store data outside a container','A volume is storage managed separately from a container\'s writable layer. It can outlive a particular container.','Container A writes a note to a named volume\nContainer A is removed\nContainer B mounts the same volume and reads the note','The volume keeps data across container replacement. It still needs a backup plan for important data.','Draw which part is the container and which part holds lasting data.','A volume is not automatically a backup and is not deleted by removing only the container.','What can persist after a container is removed?','Data in a retained volume','Every process in that container'),
B('Stop and remove only your practice container','Cleanup should target the exact resource you created. Stopping a container is separate from removing it.','docker stop club-web\ndocker rm club-web','The named practice container stops and is removed. Images and unrelated containers remain.','Run docker ps -a to confirm the named container is gone.','Do not use broad prune commands on a shared machine while learning.','Which resource is removed here?','Only club-web','Every Docker resource'),
])

C('kubernetes','Kubernetes manages groups of containers. We will learn Pods, Deployments and Services by reading small configuration files and safe inspection commands.','Read these examples without a cluster. To try commands, use only a disposable local learning cluster and the official setup guide. Do not point kubectl at a real organisation\'s cluster.','https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/kubernetes-basics/',[
B('A cluster and its tools','A cluster is a group of machines managed together. kubectl is a command-line tool that asks the Kubernetes API about that cluster.','kubectl version --client','This prints the client version without requiring a working cluster. It does not install a cluster.','Explain why having kubectl is different from having a cluster.','Check the target context before any command that changes resources.','What is kubectl?','A client tool','A complete cluster by itself'),
B('Check the current context','A context chooses a cluster, user and sometimes namespace. Read it first so you know where a command would act.','kubectl config current-context\nkubectl config get-contexts','The output shows configured contexts. A learning setup should use an explicitly chosen local context.','Locate the context for your disposable practice cluster.','Never assume the current context is a safe practice environment.','Why check context first?','To know the target cluster','To speed up every Pod'),
L('Pods hold containers','A Pod is Kubernetes\' smallest runnable unit. Containers in one Pod share some resources, such as the network address.','Pod: club-web\nContainer: web server\nPod network address: shared by containers in that Pod','Most simple examples put one main container in a Pod. A Pod is not a whole virtual machine.','Draw two Pods, each containing one web container.','Do not expect an individual Pod name or address to last forever.','What is the smallest runnable unit here?','Pod','Cluster'),
L('Deployments manage replicas','A Deployment describes how many matching Pods should run and how their template looks. A replica is one copy.','apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: club-web\nspec:\n  replicas: 2\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: club-web\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: club-web\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: web\n          image: nginx:stable','The desired state asks for two Pods matching the template. Reading this YAML does not create them.','Find the replicas field and change the planned count to 3.','The selector must match the labels on the Pod template.','How many replicas are requested?','2','1',language='yaml'),
L('Labels group resources','A label is a key-value marker attached to a resource. Selectors find resources with matching labels.','labels:\n  app: club-web\n  purpose: practice','These two labels describe the resource. A selector app=club-web can find it.','Add a label named team with a fictional value.','Labels should not hold secret or sensitive values.','What does a selector use?','Matching labels','Only the resource age',language='yaml'),
B('Inspect resources','get lists resources. describe gives a detailed human-readable view with events that can explain problems.','kubectl get pods\nkubectl describe deployment club-web','These commands read the current namespace in the chosen cluster. The Deployment must exist there for describe to succeed.','Find the namespace in your current context before reading resources.','A resource existing in another namespace does not mean it exists in the current one.','Which command gives detailed information?','describe','delete'),
L('Services give a stable route','A Service gives a stable way to reach a selected group of Pods. This example is internal to the cluster.','apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: club-web\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: club-web\n  ports:\n    - port: 80\n      targetPort: 80\n  type: ClusterIP','The Service selects Pods labelled app=club-web and forwards traffic to their port 80. It does not create a public cloud load balancer.','Compare the selector with the Deployment labels.','A Service with no matching healthy Pods cannot serve the app.','Which Service type is used?','ClusterIP','LoadBalancer',language='yaml'),
L('ConfigMaps hold ordinary settings','A ConfigMap stores non-secret configuration. An application may read selected values through environment variables or files.','apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: club-settings\ndata:\n  WELCOME_TEXT: "Hello, club"','The ConfigMap contains an ordinary welcome message. It is not a secure place for passwords.','Add a non-secret color setting.','Use the appropriate secret-management approach for real credentials; a ConfigMap is not one.','What belongs in this ConfigMap?','A non-secret welcome message','A production password',language='yaml'),
L('Resource requests and limits','Requests help schedule a container. Limits bound some resource use. CPU m means one thousandth of a CPU unit.','resources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: "100m"\n    memory: "64Mi"\n  limits:\n    cpu: "200m"\n    memory: "128Mi"','The request is 0.1 CPU and 64 mebibytes of memory. A low memory limit can cause the container to be stopped when it exceeds it.','Compare each request with its limit.','A request is not a measurement of current usage.','What does 100m CPU represent?','One tenth of a CPU unit','One hundred CPU units',language='yaml'),
L('Readiness and liveness','A readiness check asks whether a container should receive traffic. A liveness check asks whether Kubernetes should restart it.','Readiness fails -> stop sending Service traffic to that Pod\nLiveness fails repeatedly -> container may restart','The two checks serve different purposes. A temporary dependency problem should not always trigger a restart loop.','Choose which check should notice that the app is still warming up.','Do not use a restart as the answer to every readiness problem.','Which check controls receiving traffic?','Readiness','Liveness only'),
B('Read logs and rollout state','Logs show the program\'s messages. Rollout status reports progress while a Deployment changes its Pods.','kubectl logs deployment/club-web\nkubectl rollout status deployment/club-web','The first command reads selected container logs; the second observes the Deployment rollout. Output depends on the local cluster state.','Find an error message without copying any secret values.','A successful rollout does not prove every user journey works.','What does rollout status observe?','A Deployment update','A local text editor'),
L('Namespaces organise a cluster','A namespace groups namespaced resources. It helps separate teams or practice work, but is not by itself a complete security boundary.','Namespace: club-practice\nDeployment: club-web inside club-practice\nService: club-web inside club-practice','The names can coexist with similar resources in another namespace. Access controls still matter.','Write the full namespace and resource name before any cleanup plan.','Delete only a namespace you created for disposable practice; it may contain many resources.','Is a namespace alone a complete security boundary?','No','Yes'),
])

C('terraform','Terraform describes desired infrastructure in configuration files. We will use a built-in local example to learn variables, plans, state and careful review without creating cloud resources.','Use a new local folder and Terraform 1.4 or newer for the built-in terraform_data examples. Read first. Do not add cloud credentials or run apply against an unknown project.','https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials',[
B('Configuration before action','Infrastructure as code means describing resources in files. Terraform reads those files and compares the desired result with recorded state.','terraform version','This prints the local Terraform version. It does not create any infrastructure.','Explain the difference between reading a plan and applying it.','A tool that can manage infrastructure must be pointed at the intended project.','Does terraform version create resources?','No','Yes'),
L('A local resource example','terraform_data is a built-in resource that stores an input in Terraform state. It does not create a cloud machine.','terraform {\n  required_version = ">= 1.4.0"\n}\nresource "terraform_data" "greeting" {\n  input = "Hello, club"\n}','The configuration names one local state resource and gives it a greeting. Save it as main.tf in a new practice folder.','Change the greeting text before any plan.','Do not add provisioner commands to this simple practice resource.','Does terraform_data create a cloud VM?','No','Yes',language='hcl'),
B('Initialise the folder','init prepares a working directory. Backend configuration controls where state is stored; this practice keeps remote backends disabled.','terraform init -backend=false','In the built-in example, Terraform prepares the local directory. Other projects may download providers or modules during init.','Read the configuration before initialising a project.','init does not mean a configuration is safe to apply.','What does init prepare?','The working directory','A paid cloud account'),
B('Format and validate','fmt checks or rewrites formatting. validate checks whether the configuration is internally valid after needed initialization.','terraform fmt -check\nterraform validate','The commands report formatting or configuration problems. They do not apply the planned resources.','Make an indentation change and see what fmt -check reports.','Validation does not prove the planned infrastructure is appropriate or affordable.','Does validate apply resources?','No','Yes'),
L('Variables accept inputs','A variable declares an input and can provide a default. Types make the expected kind of value clear.','variable "club_name" {\n  type = string\n  default = "Reading club"\n}\nresource "terraform_data" "club" {\n  input = var.club_name\n}','The resource reads the declared string variable. In a separate practice file, avoid defining the same resource twice.','Change the default club name.','A variable default is not a safe place for a real secret.','How is this input referenced?','var.club_name','resource.club_name',language='hcl'),
L('Outputs show selected values','An output exposes a useful result from the configuration. It does not automatically make a private value safe to publish.','output "club_message" {\n  value = terraform_data.greeting.output\n}','The output refers to the greeting resource from the earlier example. Its value becomes available after the local resource has state.','Name an output that describes a non-secret value clearly.','Marking an output sensitive hides some display, but does not remove it from state.','What does an output expose?','A selected result','A new cloud region',language='hcl'),
B('Read a plan','plan describes proposed changes. It gives you a chance to review before choosing whether to apply.','terraform plan','For the new local example, the plan proposes adding the terraform_data resource. It does not apply that change.','Find additions, changes and removals in the plan summary.','Do not approve a plan you have not read, especially one that removes resources.','What does plan show?','Proposed changes','Only already-completed changes'),
L('State records managed resources','State links configuration to managed objects and their data. It can contain sensitive information, so treat it as important private project data.','Configuration: what you want\nState: what Terraform recorded\nProvider reads: what exists now','Terraform uses these sources to work out differences. Losing state can make management difficult.','Explain why state should not be committed to a public repository.','Do not edit state files manually as a normal way to change resources.','Can state contain sensitive data?','Yes','No'),
L('Local values name expressions','locals name calculated values used inside a configuration. They are not inputs supplied from outside like variables.','locals {\n  greeting = "Hello, ${var.club_name}"\n}\nresource "terraform_data" "message" {\n  input = local.greeting\n}','The local expression builds a greeting from the club_name variable.','Change the greeting words while keeping the variable reference.','Use local.greeting for a local value and var.club_name for a variable.','Which prefix reads a local value?','local.','var.',language='hcl'),
L('References express dependencies','A reference to another resource\'s result tells Terraform about a dependency. It can then choose a valid order of work.','resource "terraform_data" "first" { input = "Book" }\nresource "terraform_data" "second" { input = terraform_data.first.output }','The second resource depends on the first resource\'s output.','Draw an arrow from second to the value it needs.','File order alone is not how Terraform decides resource dependencies.','What creates the dependency here?','The output reference','The alphabetic resource name',language='hcl'),
L('Modules package configuration','A module is a group of Terraform configuration files used together. The current folder is the root module; other modules can be called from it.','Root module\n  -> local module for a repeated group of settings\nModule inputs: variables\nModule results: outputs','Inputs and outputs form the module\'s interface. Reading a module before using it helps reveal side effects and costs.','List two useful inputs for a reusable naming module.','Do not trust a downloaded module without reading what it manages.','What forms a module\'s public interface?','Variables and outputs','Only file colors'),
L('Separate review from applying','apply performs the reviewed changes. A safe workflow checks scope, target and the exact plan before changing any real resources.','Read configuration\nCheck working directory and state target\nValidate and plan\nReview proposed effects\nApply only when authorised','For this course, you can learn by reading plans. No real cloud deployment is needed.','Explain why -auto-approve is a poor starting point for a beginner.','Never run broad destruction commands against a project you do not fully own and understand.','When should real changes be applied?','After reviewing and authorising the exact change','Before reading the plan'),
])
